# Bridges the IPU6 camera into a plain /dev/video device.
#
# The IPU6 exposes ~48 raw Bayer capture nodes that no ordinary application can
# use; libcamera does the actual ISP work in software. Apps that speak PipeWire
# get a working camera already. Apps that open /dev/video directly (anything
# built on plain v4l2src — pantheon-camera, Zoom, many Electron apps) would
# otherwise grab a raw node, start the sensor (LED on) and render nothing.
#
# v4l2-relayd runs the libcamera pipeline into a v4l2loopback device ON DEMAND,
# only while something has the device open, so the camera LED and its power draw
# follow actual use.
# libcamera hands out 1920x1088 for this sensor while the relay's output caps ask
# for a fixed size. The stock output pipeline has videoconvert but no scaler, so
# caps negotiation fails silently and only the blank splash frame ever reaches
# the loopback. Scale on the input side.
#
# Output at the sensor's full height rather than downscaling to 720p: the panel
# is 2880x1800 and meeting clients send whatever the device advertises, so a
# 1280x720 cap here was the entire reason calls looked soft next to Windows.
# 1088 is the real sensor height; 1080 would mean cropping or a second rescale
# for no benefit, and clients letterbox as needed.
# NO denoise filter here, deliberately.
#
# frei0r-filter-hqdn3d was added when the picture was noisy, and measured as
# free at the time -- but that measurement was PNG-encode bound and told us
# nothing. In the real relay at 1920x1088 it collapses the pipeline: 6 fps with
# p90 inter-frame gaps of 466 ms and stalls over a second. That is what "the
# camera lags in Meet" was.
#
# Without it the same pipeline holds a solid 30 fps (gap median 33.3 ms, max
# 34.7 ms). The noise it was compensating for is largely gone anyway: retiming
# the sensor for the board's 26 MHz clock (vts_def 2244 -> 3039 in the DKMS
# driver) cut analogue gain from 193 to ~144 for the same scene.
#
# If noise ever needs attacking again, denoise AFTER a downscale, never at full
# resolution: 1280x720 with hqdn3d spatial=0.06 temporal=0.15 holds 30 fps
# (max gap 41 ms) and measures ~18% cleaner. Full resolution plus filtering is
# the one combination that does not work.
# All-GPU path: the VEBOX fixed-function unit does the colour conversion and the
# noise reduction, so no pixel work lands on the CPU at all.
#
# Two vapostproc stages, deliberately. VEBOX noise reduction is silently a NO-OP
# when the VPP input surface is RGB, and libcamera's software ISP hands us BGRx.
# The first stage converts BGRx -> NV12 on the GPU; only then does denoise do
# anything. Measured across denoise=0/16/32/48/64 on RGB input, spatial noise
# stayed flat at 5.33-5.35 -- the filter was running and doing nothing.
#
# Do NOT put a CPU filter in this chain. v4l2-relayd runs two pipelines and
# copies buffers between them with their original capture timestamps, but the
# output pipeline's only source is a live appsrc with min-latency 0, so it has
# no idea the input pipeline exists. Every millisecond spent here is undeclared
# lateness against v4l2sink's ~38 ms budget (max-lateness 5 ms + one frame).
# Exceed it and GstBaseSink drops every frame except the one per second its
# emergency clause renders anyway -- which is precisely the 1033 ms stalls that
# frei0r-filter-hqdn3d caused here. relayd's bus watch ignores WARNING messages,
# so it fails silently.
#
# Never end VIDEOSRC with a capsfilter: gst_parse_launch rejects a description
# ending in caps, relayd then calls gst_element_set_state() on NULL and only the
# splash frame ever reaches the loopback.
VIDEOSRC="libcamerasrc ! vapostproc ! video/x-raw(memory:VAMemory),format=NV12 ! vapostproc denoise=64 sharpen=0 disable-passthrough=true"
FORMAT=YUY2
WIDTH=1920
HEIGHT=1088
FRAMERATE=30/1
CARD_LABEL="Integrated Camera"
#EXTRA_OPTS=-d
